<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Royal Audiencia of Quito</h1><p class="st-meta">1563 CE – 1822 CE · capital Quito</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Spanish colonial province of the northern Andes, governed from Quito, famed for its textile workshops and baroque art, and the forerunner of Ecuador.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the Spanish conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> in 1534, the region was governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>. In 1563 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II</a> created the Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> of Quito, a high court with governing powers, covering the territory of modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Ecuador</a> and parts of southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-colombia">Colombia</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> was headed by a president. Its economy rested on textile workshops called obrajes, which used indigenous labour to produce woollen cloth sold across the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/andecavi">Andes</a>, and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">haciendas</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> was the main port and shipbuilding centre. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> became renowned for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito-school">Quito School</a> of painting and sculpture and for churches such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-society-of-jesus-quito">La Compañía</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rebellion-of-the-barrios">Rebellion of the Barrios</a> in 1765 protested new taxes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> was attached to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/viceroyalty-of-new-granada">Viceroyalty of New Granada</a> in 1739. A junta in Quito on 10 August 1809 was one of the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>, followed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-quito">State of Quito</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucre">Sucre's</a> victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pichincha">Pichincha</a> in 1822 brought Quito into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/royal-audiencia-of-quito.jpg" alt="Royal Audiencia of Quito" width="320" height="177"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Requena y Herrera · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Real_Audiencia_de_Quito_1779.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1563 CE – 1822 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Quito</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish, Quechua</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ecuador">Ecuador</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Presidency of Quito, Real Audiencia de Quito</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1692&amp;focus=royal-audiencia-of-quito" aria-label="Royal Audiencia of Quito on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2497 2187 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Royal Audiencia of Quito lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2817 2443L4910 989M2817 2443L2883 2883M2817 2443L2946 2260M2817 2443L2945 2261M2817 2443L2819 2442M2817 2443L2782 2516M2817 2443L2819 2442"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M2883 2883h0M2946 2260h0M2945 2261h0M2819 2442h0M2782 2516h0M2819 2442h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2817 2443h0"/><path class="map-dot-